WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.113 --> 00:00:02.468
(vibrant music)
(creatures vocalize)

00:00:02.468 --> 00:00:04.558
- [Paul] Welcome to Hanson Island,

00:00:04.558 --> 00:00:07.873
a remote place on the
west coast of Canada,

00:00:07.873 --> 00:00:11.905
about 10 kilometers long
and two kilometers wide.

00:00:11.905 --> 00:00:16.334
This island belongs to two
groups, the First Nations

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:17.754
and the orca.

00:00:17.754 --> 00:00:21.463
(vibrant music)
(creatures vocalize)

00:00:21.463 --> 00:00:24.750
For most of my life, I've
had the great good fortune

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.774
of living here in this
incredible environment

00:00:27.774 --> 00:00:31.101
and studying this incredible creature,

00:00:31.101 --> 00:00:35.438
and it's given me a sense
of peace in the world.

00:00:35.438 --> 00:00:39.422
I want everybody to have
that same experience.

00:00:39.422 --> 00:00:42.089
(vibrant music)

00:00:48.901 --> 00:00:51.484
(gentle music)

00:00:52.524 --> 00:00:54.031
My name is Paul Spong,

00:00:54.031 --> 00:00:57.114
and 47 years ago I was a young scientist

00:00:57.114 --> 00:00:59.610
at the University of British Columbia,

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:03.254
and part of my job was to
study an orca in captivity

00:01:03.254 --> 00:01:05.380
at the Vancouver Aquarium.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:08.886
I came to understand that
orcas are acoustic creatures,

00:01:08.886 --> 00:01:12.349
because they're using
sound to locate their prey.

00:01:12.349 --> 00:01:16.222
They get what they need
to live from sound.

00:01:16.222 --> 00:01:18.314
They should not be penned
up in concrete tanks

00:01:18.314 --> 00:01:20.901
that deprive them of
the sound of the ocean

00:01:20.901 --> 00:01:22.903
that they were born into.

00:01:22.903 --> 00:01:26.053
Ultimately, that knowledge
led me to search for a place

00:01:26.053 --> 00:01:28.853
in the wild where I could study orcas

00:01:28.853 --> 00:01:30.850
totally without interfering with them,

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.374
and that search led me to Hanson Island,

00:01:33.374 --> 00:01:35.291
where I set up OrcaLab.

00:01:37.242 --> 00:01:40.244
We built a network of
half a dozen hydrophones

00:01:40.244 --> 00:01:44.878
that cover 50 square kilometers
of the surrounding area.

00:01:44.878 --> 00:01:46.840
And they give us live audio,

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:51.007
so we can track their
movements, often 24 hours a day.

00:01:51.915 --> 00:01:53.826
61 and 60 are there, I think.

00:01:53.826 --> 00:01:54.790
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:57.341
For me, one of the
greatest experiences here

00:01:57.341 --> 00:02:01.228
is putting on a pair of
earphones and just listening.

00:02:01.228 --> 00:02:03.695
And then we have speakers in the forest,

00:02:03.695 --> 00:02:06.813
near the tents, near the
outhouse, near the camp kitchen,

00:02:06.813 --> 00:02:08.731
all of the spaces around here.

00:02:08.731 --> 00:02:10.474
(ship horn blows)

00:02:10.474 --> 00:02:13.690
One of the things you can't
help noticing and feeling

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.236
is the sudden intrusion of a boat,

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:18.281
of a vessel, of a propeller.

00:02:18.281 --> 00:02:20.159
It's jarring.

00:02:20.159 --> 00:02:23.076
(something whines)

00:02:31.628 --> 00:02:34.416
- [Researcher] What about.

00:02:34.416 --> 00:02:36.486
- [Paul] Here at OrcaLab, we listen to

00:02:36.486 --> 00:02:38.256
untold quantities of boat noise.

00:02:38.256 --> 00:02:40.132
It's really annoying.

00:02:40.132 --> 00:02:43.890
We can always walk away
from it, but whales can't.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.982
It disrupts their communication.

00:02:45.982 --> 00:02:48.446
It disrupts their ability to hunt.

00:02:48.446 --> 00:02:50.308
- They've had quite a
few breaches as well.

00:02:50.308 --> 00:02:51.141
- [Paul] Uh-huh.

00:02:51.141 --> 00:02:52.422
I think, from the point of view

00:02:52.422 --> 00:02:54.773
of the welfare of the whales,

00:02:54.773 --> 00:02:57.218
it's something that has to be dealt with.

00:02:57.218 --> 00:03:00.001
What I want to do is develop
the ability of the people

00:03:00.001 --> 00:03:03.745
to get to feel empathy for the creature.

00:03:03.745 --> 00:03:06.787
Since 2000, we've streamed live audio,

00:03:06.787 --> 00:03:10.454
so that anybody with computer
access to the internet

00:03:10.454 --> 00:03:13.928
can listen to exactly what
we listen to in our lab.

00:03:13.928 --> 00:03:15.605
- Oh, look, it's right up at the.

00:03:15.605 --> 00:03:20.062
- [Paul] What we're trying
to do is give people a taste

00:03:20.062 --> 00:03:24.229
of what it is to be an orca in
this magnificent environment.

00:03:25.185 --> 00:03:27.645
In doing that, they will come to care

00:03:27.645 --> 00:03:30.696
and want to protect nature.

00:03:30.696 --> 00:03:35.066
I believe that if I can share
what I have experienced,

00:03:35.066 --> 00:03:37.906
the world could know peace.

00:03:37.906 --> 00:03:42.073
(gentle music)
(creatures vocalize)

